[{:seon.task/name "one-listener-for-roots-life"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: web. The owner's design, confirmed 2026-11-16 (decisions: the system process is the fork point): the binary's main process is the system, single-threaded for life; it loads main by load-diff! of its held db against each commit notice, collects, and forks. The writer process is its first fork; agents and test children are its siblings and inherit only what the system process built once. Bound: a forked agent's own memory <=5 MB, no baggage (docs/cost.md#the-test-systems-lower-bound); every READY gives a fresh agent's private bytes after fork, after a collection, after one read, beside 5 MB. Web: one listener for the writer process's life, handed across a switch-over; views as reactions over the store. Deletes: bin/mcp's reconnect and next-port, seon.server/held-index, the render read-index, the holder map.\nPLAN (web, 2026-11-16).\nPRIOR WORK, searched (seon.server :hand-over and serve!, 122c4fae1b; seon.fork-server's fd passing, fork_server.clj:47 and system.clj:508; jolt.socket and jolt host-static.ss for a server socket over an fd): today a hand-over has the old writer process stop, close its port and answer {:released true :port p}, then the candidate binds p; in between, a call is refused, so bin/mcp follows :next-port and reconnects per message. Jolt has no ServerSocket over an inherited fd.\nTHE DESIGN: the system process binds 127.0.0.1:server-port once, before it forks the writer process, and keeps the listening fd for its life; each writer process it forks receives that fd (the fork server's fd passing) and serve! accepts on it; agents and test children close it at their start (they never carry a socket). A hand-over is the old writer process ending its accept loop and finishing what it admitted; the fd stays open in the system process, so the kernel's backlog holds new connections until the candidate writer process accepts on the same fd: no call is refused, and bin/mcp needs no next-port follow.\nINTERFACES: runtime, AGREED 2026-11-16: the bind is in the system process's start (the binary's main, deps' task seon.main/the-binary-main-is-the-system-process), which passes the fd in the writer process's start map as :seon.server/listen-fd; that fork keeps it (seon.lib/fork!'s :keep); agents and test children never hold it, since fork! closes every stream but 0-2, the child's own fd and those it is told to keep. deps, AGREED 2026-11-16 (its task jolt.host/a-server-socket-is-made-from-an-inherited-fd on jolt/deps): (jolt.host/server-socket-from-fd fd) answers the same ServerSocket object today's java.net.ServerSocket shim makes (.accept parks on the fiber through the same poller registration; .getLocalPort and .close as now); the writer process calls it once at start with the fd it inherited, in place of serve!'s new ServerSocket; it lands after seon.main/the-binary-main-is-the-system-process, and until then the writer process binds its own port. web: serve! takes the fd; the hand-over no longer closes it; bin/mcp's next-port path goes.\nCOST: zero per call; the hand-over's gap is zero (the backlog, :seon.config.root/server-connections deep); an agent's private bytes unchanged by web (web's data in a fresh agent ~35 KB, result/dc2a0bc9).\nPIECES, each with its proof: (1) serve! over a given fd, proved with a fd bound in the test process and passed in; (2) a hand-over that leaves the fd open, proved by a connection made during the hand-over being answered by the second server; (3) bin/mcp's next-port deleted. Views as reactions (held-index, the render read-index) follow seon.ratom/web-reads-on-the-core."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "inspect-a-kept-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Runs are kept (target/check/<time>/runs, runs/, t5/out/*.store) and are evidence. A root opens a kept store for inspection: its reconciler observes and acts on nothing (no spawn, no kill, no judge) unless released, so a run's main and branches can be queried and its agents restored without resuming its work: `bin/system --inspect STORE`, with the MCP tools working against it. Nothing exists yet (bin/system has no --inspect; the proof is unwritten)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/an-inspected-run-starts-nothing}}
 {:seon.task/name "restore-and-replay-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Datahike keeps every commit as an immutable value with its parents (``datahike.versioning/branch-history``; `branch!` accepts a commit id; `commit-as-db` loads one): on a copy of t5/out/real3.store, `d/branch!` from the middle of task-t5-edit-forms's 19 commits (335 evals) gave a branch holding exactly its 29 evals. So branching from any commit costs no copy, and an eval between commits is reached by branching from the commit before and replaying. Restore: an agent forked on a kept branch at its end state (resume's load of the branch program). Replay to step N: an agent forked from the run's basis evaluates its recorded forms 1..N in order through its own REPL, every effect answered from its recorded outcome by a replay owner (nothing fires); each result is compared with its record and a divergence recorded (nondeterminism, or a gap in the record). The debugging agent works the re-creation through seon_eval, its new instructions a task whose :evidence names the source run, branch and point. Every form's exact text is recorded (`:seon.eval/code`, the request's whole text, on every eval); it needs effect outcomes recorded whole (the model's reply is on the turn; file and other effects' answers are not). Nothing built yet."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.server/inspect-a-kept-run"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-replayed-agent-reproduces-its-recorded-results
                      seon.server-test/a-divergence-on-replay-is-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "done-reads-its-reason-once"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the guard audit of 2026-10-25 (still true in src/seon/server.clj). What exists: the :done op's c/await-db pred tests the task's outcome (not in #{:running :ready :gate}) and c/defect, and after the wait `(and (= :running (outcome db)) (c/defect db t \"seon.reconcile\"))` tests the same two facts again to choose its answer. Correct: the wait returns the reason it ended on (the pred returns the verdict or the defect it matched), and :done answers that, so one read of the facts decides. Replaces the second read. Proof to write: :done answers the defect the wait matched even when main holds a later commit."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/done-answers-defect-verdict-wait-matched-even-main}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-tools-are-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: it deletes ops the MCP tools still document and use; the owner decides whether those ops become evals before anyone builds. Owned by runtime (was Optimization) with context (owner, 2026-10-30); overlaps seon.server/a-read-only-looks. The owner, 2026-10-30: the MCP tools are thin wrappers around Seon's REPL-based eval, so an outside session works as a Seon agent does and sees what it sees; 2026-11-14: \"No more heavy machinery on the claude side. It all goes into seon and we do things the seon way going forward\" (AGENTS §0; docs/plan.md Stage 1 item 3: a session is an agent, changing code through seon_eval and seon.repl/edit!). Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: seon.server answers nine ops by op-answer methods (src/seon/server.clj:311-486): agents, eval, context, message, write_back, create, attach, prove, done; each MCP tool seon_<op> calls one (bin/mcp). Inside, a Seon agent has one tool, eval, and every capability is a function it evaluates. Design: the rule is every capability is a function an eval calls, none is only a tool. seon_eval {agent, text, code} (an agent's id or root) is the tool, through seon.repl/eval-string, origin :outside, its text the printed response a Seon agent's model reads; seon_attach stays (a session needs a process, and attach records its client as holder); agents, context, message, prove, done, create and write_back become functions evaluated on root or the agent ((my.root/agents), (my.agent/context), (my.agent/send-message ..), (my.agent/prove), (my.task/done!), (my.root/create! ..), (my.root/write-back!)), and their op-answer methods are deleted, bin/ scripts and checks calling seon_eval. Each answer adds what its caller cannot see otherwise: for a Seon agent, the problems its eval caused; for an outside client (bin/mcp stamps its client id), root's problem facts changed on main since that client's last answer, read through Datahike's history (never my.root/problems' ~600 MB recomputation). A worktree's root is one more bin/mcp entry, no new mechanism. Deletes: seven op-answer methods and their entries in seon.server/ops, the hand JSON Schema of each (with seon.server/outside-input-has-a-schema). Estimate: an op as an eval costs one eval's round trip (~3 ms measured for root's evals, 2026-11-14) instead of the op's; no new process or proc. Proofs, red first: seon.server-test/every-op-but-eval-and-attach-is-a-function-an-eval-calls (the design guard: seon.server/ops holds eval and attach only, and each removed op's answer is the answer of its my.* function evaluated through seon_eval; red today: nine ops); seon.server-test/an-outside-eval-answers-what-a-seon-agents-eval-answers (the same eval through seon_eval and through an agent's turn prints the same response)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/an-outside-eval-answers-what-a-seon-agents-eval-answers seon.server-test/every-op-but-eval-and-attach-is-a-function-an-eval-calls}}
 {:seon.task/name "outside-input-has-a-schema"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by docs/merging.md#a-landing-message-carries-its-audit its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: input from outside is parsed and dispatched with no Malli contract on its shape. seon.server/answer takes :map; respond sniffs the protocol from the line's first characters; each op's arguments are declared as JSON Schema in ops, a second schema language never used on an EDN request. seon.web's /call reads args by edn/read-string with no guard, checked first inside the web proc's step, so a malformed request is a step's throw, not a 400. seon.model's sse-reply, reply-of, wire-message(s) and request-body answer or take :map. my.root/example-txs re-states the session shape as a hand predicate (b5fabd00f0 added an arm). The change: :seon.server/request [:multi {:dispatch :op}] validated in respond, a request that is invalid answered, explained, with seon.contract/problems, and the MCP tool list's inputSchema derived from it (malli.json-schema), deleting the hand JSON Schema; the web edge reads args through the same validator and answers 400 with the problems; :seon.model/wire-reply (only the keys read) and :seon.model/wire-request; one :seon.ctx/session schema (the first transaction's agent requires :seon.agent/id and an example/ key) as seon.ctx.session/session-tx's output contract, with example-txs refusing by it, deleting the predicate. Docs: docs/model.md, docs/dashboard.md, the server's section."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-malformed-request-is-answered-with-its-problems}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-read-only-looks"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: seon.server's :context op (op-answer :context) runs seon.turn/context-block with take? = (= \"outside\" (:model env)), the agent's model, whoever asks: a read from any client of an agent an outside client works takes its delivered messages (seon.turn/deliver! take?), writes its main and merge base, and records the read as an :outside model call, the agent's turn number moving. :context is not among seon.server/own-ops, so it reads the agent it names, never asking whether the reader is the agent's holder. Reproduction (standing root, 2026-11-10): agent o21aa44adb8c304a (task seon.render/a-model-render-draws-each-turns-diff, model \"outside\", attached by one session) holds two :outside model calls on its branch, n 1 and 2, after a second session (mcp-tokens research) read its context; no message was ever sent to it, so none was lost this time.

What allowed it: a read that has an effect, chosen by the agent's settings rather than by the request. Siblings found by the sweep of seon.server's ops: (1) :context naming a task with no live agent attaches one (seon.server/attach! with the reader's :client), so a read creates an agent and makes the reader its holder; (2) every outside read writes a model call (the read entity) on the branch. :eval and :done act on the caller's own agent (seon.server/own-ops); :agents, :prove and :message read or act as named.

The change: a read never writes. :context only looks for every client (the request it would send now, made at the branch head without taking or recording), and an outside agent takes its messages when its turn opens, which seon.turn/outside-reply! already does (seon.turn/open-turn! delivers). A :context naming a task with no agent answers what attaching would show and attaches nothing (attach is its own op). Deletes: context-block's take? argument and its effects (deliver!, main-base-tx, the read's model call), and the read's recorded entity. The owner ruled (2026-11-10, Sean: \"A read never writes.\"): no record of a read is kept, on the agent's side or the reader's; the task that would have recorded reads as turns is removed.

Proofs, red first: a read by a client that does not hold the agent leaves its branch's datoms unchanged (no model call, no delivery, turn number the same); a message sent between two reads is taken by the next reply's turn and appears in the context after it; a :context naming an unattached task creates no agent. Web builds it after 2a; Context reviews what the model reads. Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.turn/an-outside-read-opens-its-turn-as-a-model-turn-does (Found by context's duplication audit, 2026-11-14 (the owner: no duplicated effort; plan item 7), both ends read [M])."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.turn-test/an-outside-read-opens-its-turn-as-a-model-turn-does seon.server-test/a-read-by-a-client-not-holding-the-agent-leaves-its-branch-unchanged
                      seon.server-test/a-message-between-two-reads-reaches-the-next-turn
                      seon.server-test/a-context-read-of-an-unattached-task-makes-no-agent}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-attach-starts-an-agent-as-a-spawn-does"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (was Optimization). Found 2026-11-11 by Data model: an attached agent's pull held no :seon.agent/max-turns while a spawned one held 40, so a render of the turn budget worked for one and not the other (a fallback in the render was refused: the defect is the start's). Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: the cause is fixed in the code: seon.server's attach! (src/seon/server.clj:130-180) spawns, then writes its agent through the same seon.task/start-tx the reconciler's spawn writes, with env = seon.reconcile/desired-env of the task (which holds :max-turns, src/seon/reconcile.clj:41) and :model \"outside\" and :holder the client; attach writes no agent fact of its own. Not yet proved: no test compares the two starts (git grep). What is left: the proof; when it is green the task is done and its entry goes with Completes:. Estimate: two agents started on one task in a test world, two pulls of their :seon.agent/* attributes (~20M ticks, two forks). The proof is also the design guard: it compares the whole attribute sets, so any fact one start writes and the other does not turns it red. Proof, red first (seen red by removing :max-turns from attach's env): seon.server-test/an-attached-agent-holds-what-a-spawned-one-holds (an attached agent's entity and a spawned one's on the same task hold the same set of :seon.agent/* attributes, but those naming the model and holder)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/an-attached-agent-holds-what-a-spawned-one-holds}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reads-bytes-are-its-own"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (was Optimization); waits on deps' Jolt task. Found 2026-11-11 (the named run of jolt/optbatch; its verdict cache held 3 reds in about 80): seon.server-test/a-later-read-draws-no-page-cold-and-costs-what-the-one-before-did went red on 'in bytes too' (later reads 4.37M to 4.47M bytes against the 1.05x bound of the read before), green alone. A cost bound that load or order turns red is a defect (docs/cost.md#what-is-measured: cost is work done). Read at jolt/runtime 6184c92521 [M]: seon.profile/measure! takes its ticks from the fiber (exact) but its bytes from seon.profile/alloc-bytes before and after (src/seon/profile.clj:246-253), the OS thread's allocation counter (profile.clj:151, Chez jolt/thread-bytes), and Jolt preempts fibers about every 1M ticks, so another fiber run on that carrier during the call is counted (confirmed by deps). The cause is the counter's owner: a per-thread count where the measure is per fiber. Design: the count is saved and restored at each fiber switch, so it is the fiber's (jolt.host/a-fibers-own-state-lives-in-its-slice, resources/seon/tasks/jolt.host.edn:13, deps'); then alloc-bytes reads the fiber's and measure! is unchanged. No second fix here: no retry, no sampling, no raised bound. Estimate: two words saved and restored per switch (~10 ticks a switch); measure! unchanged. Proof, red first: seon.profile-test/a-calls-bytes-are-its-own-with-another-fiber-allocating (one read measured with and without a second fiber allocating 10 MB on the same carrier: the same bytes within 1%; red today). Until the Jolt task lands, that proof's red is the counter's, not the read's cost."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.profile-test/a-calls-bytes-are-its-own-with-another-fiber-allocating}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-sessions-file-edit-merges-through-done"
  :seon.task/plan "Acceptance test for the owner's priority (2026-11-14: Claude sessions work, prove and merge through the system; docs/plan.md Stage 1, whose safety gate names this test with its cost proof). Owner: root with runtime. Source: notes/separation-audit.md on jolt/root, task 1. Written first, red until the path tasks land. The path, each step's mechanism and its task: (1) a scratch system makes an area agent (seon_attach exists: seon.server's attach!, src/seon/server.clj:130) [M]; (2) a file in its source is edited and imported as a transaction on its branch, the same path an agent's eval takes (Stage 1 item 4, runtime and data); (3) the agent proves by reach as messages to the effects owner's :process kind, admitted from the cores (seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer, Stage 1 item 5); (4) seon_done merges through Seon's one merge on main's program, the agent re-forked at the turn boundary (seon.merge/a-merge-of-main-costs-what-the-branch-changed, Stage 1 item 6); (5) the write-back moves git's main (Stage 1 item 7). No git worktree and no bin/land may be called. Today each step is a different mechanism and nothing walks them together [M: no such test, git grep]. Estimate, from the lower bound Stage 1 item 5 works out: a typical change proves in ~1.3 s on 18 cores (R ~ 216 tests x 21 ms / C + one 65 ms fork); this test's change reaches a handful of tests, so the whole path is a few seconds and its processes at once at most the admission's share of the cores. Proofs, red first: seon.server-test/a-sessions-edit-reaches-gits-main-through-done; its cost proof seon.server-test/a-sessions-edit-holds-no-more-processes-than-its-cores (the path's peak processes, :seon.fx/peak-processes from seon.lib/a-process-reports-the-most-processes-it-held-at-once, at most :seon.config.proofs/max-cores x :seon.config.machine/cores). Waiting on it (root, 2026-11-14): Reagent as reference code (reagent-project/reagent at fa4dc05, the commit seon.ratom ported). A new submodule breaks bin/worktree --at's base checkouts, whose reference submodules are worktrees of the main tree's module repos (bin/tree's add -A then drops the gitlink), so its pin waits for this path to replace the worktree tooling; until then the clone is a plain read-only copy at .worktrees/deps/target/reagent-clone."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-sessions-edit-holds-no-more-processes-than-its-cores seon.server-test/a-sessions-edit-reaches-gits-main-through-done}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-reply-on-the-wire-is-made-one-way"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by context's duplication audit, 2026-11-14 (the owner: no duplicated effort; plan item 7), both ends read [M]. Web's. seon.server (server.clj:343) repeats seon.agent/on-the-wire's plain-value rule inline with a different key set (:text :ex :recorded :main), so root's reply and an agent's carry different keys; seon.repl and seon.turn already call on-the-wire. Change: the server calls seon.agent/on-the-wire. Deletes: the inline rule. Proof: root's and an agent's reply to the same eval carry the same keys."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-reply-on-the-wire-is-made-one-way}}
 {:seon.task/name "connections-park-on-fibers-and-ops-run-bounded"
  :seon.task/plan "Web's, with runtime-2. Root's order, item 4: fibers for the server's connections. Read 2026-11-14 22:00 [M].\nWhat exists:\n- seon.server/serve! runs an accept OS thread, plus one OS thread per connection, with no bound (server.clj:634-639).\n- Each connection reads a line, answers it inline (respond → answer → op-answer) and writes the reply.\n- Ops read the store inline (19 d/ reads in server.clj), and :eval as root evaluates its code on the connection's thread. A blocking read in one (stdin) held that connection 30 min (docs/server.md :eval).\n- Jolt's java.net.Socket and ServerSocket come from jolt.socket (host-static.ss:1472) and park on a fiber.\nWhy not just swap the threads for fibers: a fiber is bound to its carrier for life (docs/flow.md, the model). A blocking call on a fiber, whether an LMDB read through FFI or a blocking eval, strands every fiber on that carrier, Flow procs included. So a blocking eval on a connection fiber is worse than on its own thread.\nChange:\n- The socket IO is the edge, so it moves to fibers: the accept loop and each connection's reads and writes park (a/io-thread). The connections are bounded by a spec'd :seon.config.server/max-connections through a token channel (the primer's admission).\n- Each op runs off the connection, as a request to an owner proc on a :mixed worker with its declared bound (seon.fx's pattern, or the api's ask with its own bound), answered back to the connection's fiber. A run past its bound answers :seon.bound/reached, typed.\n- That bound is root's eval's request bound, which runtime-2 planned in seon.server, so one change covers both.\nEstimate:\n- n = live connections (bin/mcp relays and dashboards, ~10).\n- Today: an OS thread a connection, ~87 KB and 8 µs to spawn. After: a fiber, ~6 KB and 2.2 µs (docs/flow.md, go/thread).\n- A request adds one ask and answer per op, a few µs against ops of ms.\n- No carrier is ever held by an op; a stuck op ends at its bound.\nProofs:\n- seon.server-test/a-blocking-op-holds-no-other-connection: an eval that blocks doesn't delay another connection's :agents answer, and ends :seon.bound/reached at its bound;\n- seon.server-test/connections-past-the-bound-wait-their-turn.\nSplit, to agree with runtime-2: web builds the fiber edge and admission; runtime-2 the op owner and bound, if it takes it.\n\nSettled with runtime-2, 22:05: web builds both halves, and runtime-2 reviews the op owner's lines.\n(1) No new owner pattern: an op is a request to the effects owner, seon.fx/owner-proc plus worker-proc on a :mixed worker, under a :seon.fx kind for server ops (the one mechanism, docs/flow.md#effects-one-owner-and-its-workers-per-kind).\n(2) The bound is work, not a wall clock: ticks and bytes through jolt.host/run-interruptible on the worker, a run past it answering :seon.bound/reached. A read blocked in a foreign call is ended by deps' parking fix, never by a timer (flow's ruling: a wall bound on root's eval is a clock beside the cause). To agree with flow before building.\n\nAgreed with flow, 21:56, two conditions [M, flow's probe at root's REPL]:\n(a) On a :mixed worker's OS thread *ns* falls back to clojure.core, so a def would be interned there; with bound-fn it is root.repl again, and sys travels with root's eval fn (it is no var). So the worker runs a (bound-fn ...) made when the request is taken, or root's eval fn binds *ns* and its dynamic vars itself. Proof added: a def from a server op lands in root.repl.\n(b) A parked slurp burns no ticks, so a ticks bound never ends it. The cause is fixed at its source: an op's *in* is bound to the request's own input, empty unless the client sends some, so a slurp of stdin answers EOF at once (my 30-min hang). A client that drops ends its op through its connection's EOF (rule 3), with no timer. Only a foreign read that blocks waits for deps' parking fix.\nProofs added: seon.server-test/a-def-from-a-server-op-lands-in-root-repl, seon.server-test/an-ops-stdin-is-its-requests-input.\n\nAPPROVED by root 2026-11-15 17:0x (the Stage 1 safety item: the MCP relay answers each call on its own; flow reviews the token's home). The fiber edge is built; what remains: (1) each op runs under seon.intr/run in seon.server/answer (the one place every op passes) with a declared work bound, :seon.config.fx/bounds :server-op {:seon.bound/ticks :seon.bound/bytes}, its reason stated (the heaviest legit op, :context on root's session, 6.8B ticks / 35.8 GB [result/b9a43662]); root's own :eval stays unbounded, its docstring saying why (a whole-program load) and what ends it (its interrupt, end-all at stop); a run past the bound throws the typed :seon.repl/ticks-spent, answered by respond's edge as :seon.server/defect with it under :data, and the server goes on. (2) seon_interrupt: a caller stops its own op by its request's token (home: flow's answer pending). (3) serve-connection!'s deref then ends by the op's answer, its bound, its interrupt or end-all. MEASURED: seon.intr/run costs 321 ticks and 2.3 KB an op on a :server-op worker; a never-ending loop with a 100k budget stops typed [result/b1cdc6c2]; 1 of 16 workers busy at a time [result/dbf6c1ba]. RED (committed on jolt/web): seon.server-test/an-op-past-its-bound-answers-its-caller-and-the-server-goes-on. Plan: the web session's notes, web-relay-every-call-answers-plan-2026-11-15.\n\nFLOW's review, YES with three changes (notes flow-review-of-web-relay-plan-2026-11-15.md): (1) seon_interrupt runs inline on the connection fiber (as seon_inbox does), to the :server-op owner's own port, never queued behind the ops it should stop. (2) The missing end is the caller going away: serve-connection! waits on the op's answer OR its connection's end, and at the end interrupts the op (or drops it from the queue); this also ends a queued op's wait while unbounded root evals hold every worker. CAVEAT [read, bin/mcp]: the relay half-closes after sending (shutdown(s,1)), so a read sees EOF at once; a caller that went away shows as a failed write or a full close, which is what to detect. (3) The token lives in the owner's state, made at dispatch and handed to the worker with the request (workers share one channel); an interrupt names the client (seon/client), not a request id. ALSO (root, owner's ruling decisions.md 2db6386065): bin/mcp call's {intent, code} -> :evals rewrite (bin/mcp:36) moves into seon.server's :eval op; bin/seon-import's perl scrape of :agent and :task (its lines 19-20, 24) becomes one op taking {path} that calls my.branch/import!.\n\nBUILT on jolt/web (web, after flow's review): (1) seon.server/answer runs each op under seon.intr/run with :seon.config.fx/bounds :server-op (110B ticks, 376 GB: ten times :context's heaviest measured request, 11B ticks / 37.6 GB [result/a444fa99]); root's :eval under the token with no bound. (2) seon.fx/owner-proc: a request with :seon.fx/stop-key gets its token (jolt.host/make-interrupt) at dispatch, kept in the owner's :stoppable until its outcome; a {:seon.fx/stop k} on :ask stops every match (queued: answered :seon.fx/stopped and dropped; running: seon.intr/stop!, which sets the run and every run nested on its thread and interrupts the thread). intr/run takes the asker's token and checks only its own thread's stopped runs (another worker's stopped run no longer refuses this one's start). (3) seon_interrupt (:interrupt) stops its client's ops; MCP's notifications/cancelled stops {:client :id}; both inline on the connection's fiber (seon.server/line-parts reads each line once, respond takes it). DEVIATION from flow's (2), sent to flow: EOF is not the caller's end [M: macOS nc and bin/mcp half-close after sending, then read the answer; a full close after a half-close sends nothing the server sees], so the caller's end is the protocol's own cancellation. (4) The :eval op takes plain {intent, code} on an agent as one eval (bin/mcp's perl rewrite and agent-reply's refusal deleted); :import {path} imports onto the checkout's held agent (bin/seon-import is a one-call wrapper). Proofs: seon.server-test/an-op-past-its-bound-answers-its-caller-and-the-server-goes-on (rewritten: the bound set in root's process, where the server reads it), seon.server-test/a-caller-stops-its-own-op.\n Flow agreed the deviation. The residue, named: a client killed outright (SIGKILL) sends no cancel; every bounded op still ends at its bound, but root's :eval, with no work bound, runs until seon_interrupt or root's stop: the one way a dead client's op outlives it. Root's rule for bin/ scripts (design the job left, never port the script): the jobs left were \"a shell caller sends one eval\" (the :eval op takes code and intent, as root's already did) and \"the edit hook imports a file onto its checkout's agent\" (:import {path}, which reads the checkout from the caller's client); no perl logic moved inside.\n\n\nPLAN, ITEM 4 (owed from the relay plan, root's reviewer named it): root's debug page shows each running server op, its client, its MCP request id and its age. PRIOR WORK, read: seon.fx/owner-proc's :stoppable (each request in flight that its asker may stop, with :taken-ns and its :seon.fx/stop-key), its :ping-map-fn (fx.clj:1962: busy, in-flight, queued); seon.system's stall evidence (system.clj:288-300) reads each effect owner's ping as :fx {pid {:busy :in-flight :queued :done}}; root's debug page draws root's process (seon.render.ui/process-tile from seon.render.proc/process-value). THE CHANGE: the ping adds :running, a vector of {:client :id :since-ns} read from :stoppable (derived, nothing stored); the stall evidence carries it; process-tile lists each running server op with its age, so a root eval held for minutes is seen and stopped (seon_interrupt). COST: O(running ops) a ping, at most server-op-workers (16) rows; lower bound the same. Deletes nothing; no functionality removed. PROOF, red first: seon.render-test/roots-debug-page-shows-each-running-server-op (a :server-op owner state with one stoppable request draws its client and id on the process tile).\n CORRECTION BEFORE CODE (web, on reading both ends): the render proc never pings (seon.system/stall-state pings only at a hang check, system.clj:302-312), and process-value reads the api's view (render/proc.clj:66-86, seon.system/view-numbers). So the running set reaches the page as a message, not a ping: the :server-op owner, at each dispatch and outcome of a stoppable request, puts {:op :server-ops :running [{:client :id :since-ns}]} on its :out (the api's :back), the api keeps it in its view (one key, replaced whole), and process-value passes it to process-tile. Still derived (the owner's :stoppable), still at most 16 rows; one message a stoppable dispatch and outcome. The ping keeps :running too, for stall-state's evidence.\n"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/connections-past-the-bound-wait-their-turn seon.server-test/a-caller-stops-its-own-op seon.server-test/a-blocking-op-holds-no-other-connection seon.server-test/an-ops-stdin-is-its-requests-input seon.server-test/an-op-past-its-bound-answers-its-caller-and-the-server-goes-on seon.server-test/a-def-from-a-server-op-lands-in-root-repl}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-message-reaches-the-session-that-drives-its-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "APPROVED by the owner via root, 2026-11-15 (parts 1-4 of flow's messaging plan; part 5, sessions messaging each other through Seon instead of chat, waits until 1-4 are proved on the standing system). The owner: a Claude session drives an agent by hand through MCP, and messages must reach it there.\nFOUND [M, standing system, 11:31-11:37 by date, probe agent ba18c9e9bd81a184, cancelled]:\n- A seon_message to an attached agent never reaches its session. outside-reply!'s merge-main! brings the entity onto the branch (tx 536892235), so it counts as delivered (seon.agent/undelivered 0), and seon_eval's answer carries nothing.\n- seon_context shows it as one line of 14 KB, with nothing marking it new.\n- The wake waits in the reconciler's :late until the task parks, and an attached task never parks.\n- An attached task has no :seon.task/started-by, and a reply's message-md is only :seon.model-call/reply, so nothing the agent says reaches its driver.\n- seon_message from a session is written :from \"user\" (the owner).\nCAUSE: messaging was built for a model driver: delivered means on the branch, and the reader is the next model request. A session has neither the request nor a wake, and who drives isn't data (the :model \"outside\" string).\nPRIOR WORK: seon.task/message-tx and undelivered (task.clj:551); seon.agent/deliver! (agent.clj:467); seon.system/message-write and the writer answer's :wake (system.clj:355, :747); the api's pend/reply! (system.clj:461); seon.turn/outside-reply! and context-block (turn.clj:358, :397; the driver's last read is its :seon.model-call/origin :outside record); seon.agent/a-reply-is-a-message-with-a-to (unblocked by part 3); driver-as-config (flow-agent-repl-design).\nTHE CHANGE (nothing new; each extends the mechanism named):\n1. (flow) outside-reply! and context-block run the one deliver!, and each answer leads with :messages: those placed on the branch after the driver's last outside call's basis t. Derived, no read state; it covers messages a merge brought.\n2. (web: the op; flow: the api pend) seon_inbox agent=X answers at once if any message is unread. Otherwise the api holds it as a pend keyed by the agent's key, answered by the same :wake the writer's answer carries. It's an outside wait with a declared, spec'd bound, :seon.config.server/inbox-wait-ms, ending in :seon.bound/reached. A session runs bin/mcp call inbox in the background, and its exit is the notification. Idle cost: one pend entry, no fiber.\n3. (flow, with data's agent-scoped config key) attach! writes :seon.task/started-by as the attaching session's agent, and the driver as data, :seon.config.agent/driver :session. tell-tx's end notices, an agent's send-message to its driver, and a reply's message-md (a message entity to started-by) then reach it through 1 and 2.\n4. (web) seon_message's :from is the connection's agent, never the signed-in user unless it's the page.\nRulings (root): a thread is a :seon.message/task ref, ordered by tx, with no reply-to chain (data names it; context shows it); an approval is a review task named in :seon.task/needs, not an approved-by attribute.\nDecided (root, 2026-11-15, by 12:25 by date): (1) seon_inbox, the op a session calls, keeps its declared bound :seon.config.server/inbox-wait-ms (600000, spec'd) and ends with the typed :seon.server/no-message: its producer is usually a person or another session (the world outside) and its waiter an outside client that must never block forever (root, by the owner's rule split by PRODUCER; the owner approved part 2 with a bound). The in-system wait under it, seon.system/await-message, has no clock: it ends at the commit writing the message or root's stop. (2) A session's agent key is its AREA name (\"flow\", \"web\", \"data\", \"tests\", \"runtime\", \"deps\", \"context\"; root's \"root\"), never a generation (\"web-2\"), so a fresh session after a reset drives the same agent and its history continues; web builds attach-at-start with part 4. (3) Part 3 lands :seon.task/started-by first, without the driver key, which waits for data's config scopes (after F), with no interim second resolver. (4) A land's status becomes a store fact with F (merges through seon_done); until then status lines are still sent. Part 1 is built (jolt/flow a95f5aedd2, red then green) and waits at root's land gate.\nPARTS 2-4, flow's review of web's plan (notes web-messaging-parts-2-4-plan-2026-11-15.md), YES with four changes, taken: (1) one wake rule: on every writer answer the api derives the recipients from the commit's :seon.message/to datoms (O(d)), deleting message-write's :wake list and seon.reconcile/write's ks, so the reconciler's tell-tx and landed-messages wake an inbox too; (2) no \"since\" argument: seon_inbox answers seon.task/undelivered (main vs X's branch) and delivers by writing them onto X's branch (delivery is the fact); (3) the bound is a put-after! deadline, step/cancel!'d at the wake and answered through step/answered; (4) the api's stop end-alls :inbox. SPLIT: flow builds the api side, `seon.system/await-message [sys k]`: a promise answered {:woken k} when a commit carries a message to agent k, or the typed :seon.server/inbox-quiet at :seon.config.server/inbox-wait-ms (600000), the pend :inbox {k [p ...]} in the api's state. web builds onto it: the seon_inbox op (await-message, then undelivered and delivery), connection-agent, :from, the area session agent.\nCOST [M result/b45faee3]: message-tx is 6 datoms, 82,300 ticks speculated; undelivered is ~10.5k ticks a message (a seek, an entity and a lookup). (1) is O(m_new) seeks a reply, as a model turn's delivery; (2) is O(1) a wake (one map lookup), ~100 B a parked wait. Lower bound: one write per message, and one seek of the new messages and one hop per wake.\nDeletes: the session's poll-and-diff of seon_context (by 1 and 2); the reconciler's :late entry for a running session-driven agent (the pend answers the wake); the \"outside\" model-name tests, 5 readers (by the driver config). No functionality removed.\nEach land carries COST (estimate beside measured), Deletes, and its proofs red first. Plan with all areas' lines: notes flow-messaging-plan-2026-11-15.md. BUILT (flow, jolt/flow 4082cf05e0, red then green): seon.system/await-message [sys k], the api's ::inbox, woken by any main commit's :seon.message/to ops (wake-inbox), end-all at stop; proof seon.server-test/an-inbox-wait-answers-when-a-message-is-written. Part 1 LANDED 5ea1bbd49e, verified live (probe p2f45f2aac9c4ffd). THE PERSON (the owner, via web and root): a person is an agent, on the same path. To a person: a message entity to their agent (the chat page renders its :seon.message/_to). From a person: the same entity, :from by connection-agent's signed-in fallback. Replies: message-md to :seon.task/started-by (part 3), the person when they started the task. Waiting: a session with seon_inbox, the page by the reactive core; one fact, one delivery. PROGRESS UPDATES: a message with :seon.message/kind :progress and :seon.message/task, to started-by (both root's agent and the person's when root's session started it: one entity, two refs), O(1) a write; a :progress message wakes no turn; the page's waiting line shows the task's newest :progress, a tap all (web). Schema (data): :seon.message/kind (:note default, :progress), :seon.message/task (ref). RESPONSIVENESS: a person's message wakes at once; its model call first at the :model owner by :prio (runtime's person-first, flow reviews); the person can eval beside a running turn (the REPL session part). PARTS 2-4 MOVED TO FLOW (owner's ruling via root; APPROVED plan): ONE event, the commit of a message to agent k, read by three readers with no second mechanism: a parked Seon agent (the reconciler's :wake, exists), a Claude session (seon_inbox), the person (the page's render, web reviews). seon_inbox runs OFF the :server-op workers (16 OS threads [M default.edn:1228-1231]): the connection's fiber (256, ~6 KB each) parks on seon.system/await-message's promise; a put-after! of :seon.config.server/inbox-wait-ms (600000) delivers :seon.server/no-message to the same promise (an outside wait, the owner's rule); then the undelivered messages are written onto k's branch and answered. Session agents by AREA key are made at a client's first op (session-agent-tx k: root-agent-tx generalized). connection-agent: the client's area agent, else the agent it holds (:holder), else the signed-in person; it is :from (part 4) and attach!'s :seon.task/started-by (part 3). COST: per message one write plus O(1) wakes; a waiting session one :avet seek a commit (~10k ticks); connection-agent one seek, the running scan only on a miss (43k ticks [web M result/d690175f]). Proofs red first per part: a-message-from-a-connection-is-from-its-agent, a-session-agent-is-made-for-its-area-once, an-inbox-op-answers-its-message-without-holding-a-server-op-worker, an-attached-agents-end-is-told-to-its-session. Root's correction to an earlier cost: a plan is ~31M ticks warm since landing 12 [tests M result/a57de360], not 263-874M. DECIDED (root, after flow's proposal; replaces 'agent keyed by its AREA name' and session-agent-tx): a session's agent is the agent attached to the task session/<area>, made once from the :user template (seon.template, as request/root is: it parks between messages and is never done, a property of its template, no special case), re-attached by attach!'s existing agent-key continuity (task/agent-key); attach! records the client as :holder, which connection-agent reads; seon_message and seon_inbox address it by :task. No reserved agent keys; root-agent-tx untouched (the system-entity task's).\nPART 3 AND ATTACH-AT-START, flow's plan (2026-11-15 16:3x by date; web handed attach-at-start to flow, root moved parts 2-4 to flow). For root to read before code.\nPRIOR WORK (searched: git grep session/ src and tasks, none built; seon.template, seon.system/root-agent-tx, seon.server/attach! and connection-agent, seon.task/agent-key, seon.reconcile/tell-tx): request/root is made by root-agent-tx as (tpl/request-tx db (tpl/from (tpl/templates :user) decl)) parked (system.clj:1278); attach! records the client as env :holder (server.clj attach!); connection-agent reads :holder over task/running and its :parked (part 4); task/agent-key continues a task's agent key across attaches (task.clj:410); tell-tx tells :seon.task/started-by how a task ends (reconcile.clj:810); data's b2bb01e406 moves attach!'s start before the spawn, into one start fn.\nTHE CHANGE (each extends the mechanism named; nothing beside them):\n1. seon_attach takes an optional :template: when its task does not exist, attach! makes it from that template (tpl/from, tpl/request-tx, as root-agent-tx makes request/root), then attaches it. A session's first act is seon_attach task=session/<area> template=user (its launch; docs/users.md#messages says so). The agent parks between requests and is never done: the :user kind's property, no special case. Re-attach after a reset continues its key (task/agent-key).\n2. connection-agent prefers, among the agents the client holds, the one whose task is of kind :user (a template property, never a name prefix): the session's own agent, not a task it attached later.\n3. attach! writes [:db/add task :seon.task/started-by [:seon.agent/key by]] in its start (data's start fn), by = connection-agent before this attach, only when the task has no started-by (who asked stays, task.clj:618) and by is not the task's own agent. tell-tx then reaches the session through seon_inbox (part 2).\nCOST [M result/a00e44fe, root warm, twice equal]: task/running 44,973 ticks (3 running), task/agent-key 19,279; connection-agent ~2 scans ~90k ticks, once per attach, beside a fork of millions; one datom. Lower bound: one :avet seek if :holder were a fact (follow-up, not here).\nDELETES: nothing removed; the session's message-less attach (no started-by) gains its driver.\nPROOFS (red first): a-session-agent-is-made-for-its-area-once (two attaches of session/x template=user: one task, one key); an-attached-agents-end-is-told-to-its-session (a session attaches session/x, then task y; y's end is a message to session/x's agent, read by seon_inbox).\nORDER: after data's b2bb01e406 lands (its start fn), rebased onto it.\nAPPROVED (root, 2026-11-15, as read): part 3 + attach-at-start; build after data's attach! start-before-spawn (b2bb01e406, with f723ca5fd9's exact undo of a refused start, flow approved) lands."
