 {:seon.task/name "an-eval-at-root-always-answers-its-asker"
  :seon.task/plan "flow, filed 2026-11-15 by root's order; investigation after part 1 of a-message-reaches-the-session-that-drives-its-agent. Class: every wait ends (docs/errors.md#nothing-crashes-and-nothing-hangs).\nEVIDENCE [root's report; not yet read in logs]: three seon_eval agent=root calls never answered, though their own work is fast. context ~08:58: MCP gave up after 1,800 s (sorting a branch db's schema keys). web ~09:00: 28 min (code that takes 6 ms). web 11:20: task-file-text, interrupted.\nTHE PATH, both ends read [M, server.clj:98-118 at jolt/flow 561c4ba7b0]: bin/mcp's relay sends the request; seon.server's connection reads it on its own OS thread (one per connection, serve!); op-answer :eval → root-eval runs repl/eval-string inline on that thread, then sends the record with c/transact-main! without waiting; the answer goes back to the connection, then to the relay.\nHYPOTHESES, each with what settles it:\n(a) [H] the eval itself parks or blocks: a store read in a foreign call on the connection thread, or a lock. Settled by a stall capture or fiber dump at the time; root.log around 08:58, 09:00 and 11:20.\n(b) [H] the answer is written but the relay's per-message child never reads it, or reads another request's. Settled by the relay's log and the connection's write.\n(c) [H] the connection thread waits on something the op asked of the api. root-eval asks nothing synchronously, but the eval's forms may deref (c/call!, @(c/branch-db ...)). Settled by the api's pend table at the time (seon.step/pend rids with no answer).\nPRIOR WORK: seon.step/every-request-is-answered-at-each-end-of-its-holder (seon.step.edn:23, the answerer's side: one end-all); seon.step/end-all (b3cd1823c4); the server's fiber task seon.server/a-blocking-op-holds-no-other-connection (an op past its bound answers :seon.bound/reached, which would end (a) for the asker).\nREAD SO FAR [M, flow, read around 11:50 by date]: (1) Inherited sockets are ruled out: a forked child closes every socket it inherited first (jolt.async.remote/close-streams-but!, read from /dev/fd), and a spawn uses POSIX_SPAWN_CLOEXEC_DEFAULT (jolt process.ss:1161). (2) A lingering old root is ruled out: after the 11:17 restart, only 77484 (11:19:37) holds the port (lsof). (3) The relay's child reads to EOF (bin/mcp: shutdown(s,1); local $/; <$s>), so it answers only when root closes the connection. (4) The server side has one unbounded wait: serve-connection!'s @(c/effect! sys :server-op {:run run}) (server.clj). An op whose answer the effects owner loses, or that never ends, holds the connection open forever, and the relay never answers its JSON-RPC error. (5) root.log is rewritten at each start (runs/root.log, 25 bytes at 11:20), so the 08:58 and 09:00 cases left no evidence: a restart erases a hang's log. The leading hypothesis is (4): the deref is the wait that must end, by the op's own answer, the owner's typed refusal, or root's stop (seon.step/end-all at the owner).
PROOF (red first, once the waiter is found): seon.server-test/an-eval-at-root-whose-answer-is-lost-is-answered-with-its-error. Its exact shape follows the cause.\nCOST: O(1) per request (one pend entry and its answer); the lower bound is one answer per request."
