 {:seon.task/name "a-message-is-delivered-only-by-its-delivery"
  :seon.task/plan "Owners: data (the attribute, seon.agent/deliver!, built on jolt/data), flow (the inbox side, reviews). Found by root, 2026-11-16 04:56: deps re-attached session/deps and its inbox never showed root's 04:41 message (t 536912836, :ok true). CAUSE [M result/b0e98583, result/c45c063e]: ```seon.task/undelivered``` counts a message delivered when the agent's branch holds the message entity, so a message brought down by a merge of main, or held at a fork, reads as delivered though no inbox showed it. PRIOR WORK: seon.task/undelivered (the one reading of delivery), seon.agent/deliver! (the one delivery), seon.server/inbox-messages, seon.ctx/placed-messages (a model agent's page). DESIGN, agreed data+flow 2026-11-16 05:0x: delivery is its own fact, :seon.agent/delivered on the AGENT, a set of refs to :seon.message/fact, written only by deliver! on the agent's branch (schema entry beside :seon.message/to in seon.facts.edn: [:set {:seon.db/to [:seon.message/fact] :description \"The messages delivered to this agent by seon.agent/deliver!, a ref each\"} :seon.db/ref], optional in :seon.db/agent-fact). Not :seon.message/delivered-to on the message: the merge keys conflicts by [:datum e a], so N recipients writing one message's attribute on N branches conflict on their merges to main; the agent's own attribute has one writer. undelivered seeks [agent :seon.agent/delivered m] per message (eavt), as today one lookup a message. CUTOVER, exact: a message a branch already holds counts delivered once only when deliver! wrote it there (its :seon.message/id datom's tx has :seon.tx/branch = the agent's branch; a merged-down or forked-in one carries main's tx), so deps' lost note comes back. PAGE: a model agent's page places a message by the tx that linked its :seon.message/to on the branch (:at, seon.ctx/placed-messages); re-delivery adds only the delivered datom, :at unchanged, so it is shown once; only a session's inbox shows it again, once (the fix). COST: O(m) seeks on main and O(m) lookups on the branch, m the agent's messages; one datom a delivery; the cutover one seek and one tx pull a held message, once. Deletes: undelivered's presence rule and its fork and merge cases."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-message-merged-down-from-main-is-still-in-the-inbox}
  :seon.task/needs #{}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.task{undelivered {:n 30, :order :linear, :ticks 300000, :bytes 3000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-call-is-answered-across-a-restart"
  :seon.task/plan "STATUS (web, 2026-11-16): the relay half is on main (ad7465db5b: bin/mcp sends a call answered :seon.system/restarting with next-port once to that port; proof seon.check-test/a-relay-follows-a-draining-root-to-its-candidate). The server half waits on runtime: how the takeover gives the :server-op effects owner the candidate's port (proposed: one message {:op :drain :next-port p} on its in-port before ::flow/drain; the owner then answers each new request with the restarting error, the server writes it at server.clj's owner-refusal path). Asked of runtime, no answer yet.\nOwner: web. Plan item 17 (docs/plan.md#the-order): a restart loses no call. A call in flight when root is replaced gets its real answer, and the next call reaches the new root; no caller re-asks (a re-ask is a retry at the caller, AGENTS.md section 3).\nPRIOR WORK, searched (git grep restarting, :seon.system/ended, takeover in src, resources and docs; this task's history in the store and in git before 84c7b309f5): bin/mcp opens one connection per message and reads runs/root.port at each message (bin/mcp, docs/server.md#mcp), so a new root on root.port takes the next call unchanged. A call cut today gets the relay's untyped \"root answered nothing (it stopped mid-request?)\". seon.system/stop! closes the api with calls in flight. :seon.system/ended (seon.problems.edn) is the typed end prove reads (prove.clj:61-65). The mechanism that makes the cut impossible is runtime's switch-over, seon.system/an-update-loses-nothing: a new root boots beside the old on the one store, the old admits nothing new, finishes what it admitted by its own bounds, hands main's writer over (option b, settled 2026-11-14: the old root writes nothing to main after the handover) and exits. Its L2 step 1 makes the server answer a new op typed :seon.system/restarting.\nWEB'S PART, the S4 drain contract (seon.system/an-update-loses-nothing L3 S4 and WHAT THE PERSON SEES), agreed with flow 2026-11-16: (1) the server is told to drain by one message carrying :next-port, the candidate's port, sent by runtime's takeover step, never a fact it polls; (2) from then each new request is answered at once on its connection's fiber, before any op runs, {:seon.error/type :seon.system/restarting :next-port p} (MCP: the JSON-RPC error's data), :interrupt excepted (it still stops its caller's own ops); (3) a parked :inbox wait is no admitted op: the drain ends it (seon.step/end-all) answered :seon.system/restarting with :next-port; (4) admitted ops run to their answer, :seon.flow/held being the ops in flight; root's :eval is held to the :server-op bound, then interrupted and answered :seon.server/interrupted, so the drain's bound is the :server-op bound; (5) bin/mcp, answered :seon.system/restarting with :next-port, sends the same line to that port once and relays its answer: safe because (2) answers before any op runs, which the proof keeps. Built with its first caller, the takeover step: no capability lands before it. Nothing is built before the switch-over's handover is on main: no interim mechanism beside it (decisions.md 2026-11-16).\nCOST: no work added a call: the relay already reads root.port per message; the server's stop of accept is one close. Lower bound: zero per call; the handover's cost is runtime's.\nPROOF, red first: seon.server-test/a-call-in-flight-across-a-takeover-gets-its-answer: a stand-in old root holding call 1 is told to drain with :next-port; call 1 gets its real answer from the old root, call 2 is answered :seon.system/restarting before any op runs, and the relay sends it once to the new root, which answers it. Red today: stop! closes the api and call 1 gets the relay's untyped error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-call-in-flight-across-a-takeover-gets-its-answer}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.system/an-update-loses-nothing"}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.server{serve! {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 10000, :bytes 10000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-agents-index-is-a-reaction-over-main"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: web; runtime reviews the core side. The owner's ruling (decisions.md 2026-11-16): in-process temporary data is derived through seon.ratom reactions. PRIOR WORK, searched (git grep defonce and atom in src/seon/server.clj; seon.ratom's track and make-reaction; seon.fork-server/index-at-fork): seon.server/index-now (server.clj:83-96) keeps the last reconciler index it made of each root's main in a process-global atom, held-index (server.clj:78), keyed by conn and advanced by hand from its commit to now (seon.reconcile/index's incremental arity); seon.fork-server keeps its own copy the same way. Two hand-kept holders of one derived value.\nTHE CHANGE: the index is one reaction over main's store (seon.ratom/track of seon.reconcile/index on the store's db, advanced by the commit's moved datoms as every reader is), read by :agents and by the fork server alike; held-index and index-now's hand advance are deleted, and the fork server's copy with them if it reads the same reaction.\nCOST: lower bound per commit, the index's incremental step over the commit's moved task datoms, O(d); today the same step, run lazily at the next :agents call. Estimate 50k ticks a commit that moves a task, 0 otherwise.\nPROOF, red first: seon.server-test/the-agents-index-is-a-reaction-over-main: after a commit moving one task, the index :agents reads equals a cold seon.reconcile/index of main, and seon.server holds no atom of it. Red today: held-index."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/the-agents-index-is-a-reaction-over-main}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.ratom/a-commit-reaches-only-the-readers-of-what-it-moved"}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.server{index-now {:n 1, :order :linear, :ticks 50000, :bytes 200000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-message-is-read-by-value-and-marks-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: flow, with web (the op). The owner, 2026-11-16 (decisions.md 2026-11-16, via root's design round): read/unread is fragile; an agent looks at its messages without marking anything. It replaces the delivery design (data and flow, 2026-11-16 05:0x): flow built :seon.agent/delivered on jolt/flow 6ca9f2aa27, proved it on 1,648 reached tests, and reverted it unlanded, since it is such a mark.\nCAUSE of the four open inbox tasks (an-inbox-answers-a-message-once, an-inbox-on-an-ended-agent-says-so, an-inbox-wait-holds-no-state-of-its-own, a-message-is-delivered-only-by-its-delivery): a message's reading is a write onto the agent's branch (seon.agent/deliver!, agent.clj:460), which only a live process can make, and its presence there, which a merge of main also makes, is read as the delivery (seon.task/undelivered, task.clj:550): 30-54 of each session's messages were held by merges alone [M result/affef48f].\nPRIOR WORK: seon.task/message-tx and undelivered; seon.agent/deliver! and undelivered; seon.server/inbox-answer and inbox-deliver; seon.system/await-message and the api's ::waits; seon.ctx/placed-messages (a turn's messages by link tx); seon.reconcile/wake, settle-late, boot-wakes (the :late index).\nDESIGN (web and flow, then the owner via root, 2026-11-16: through Flow, never by filtering commits): (1) ONE WRITE PATH: every message to an agent on main is written by seon.system/message-write (system.clj:381), a fact first through the writer, flushed before it is answered, so no channel holds the only copy. Today four other sites write messages on main: system.clj:265, reconcile.clj:856 (asked), reconcile.clj:1073 (a result told), task.clj:547 (landed, through the reconciler's write); each becomes a message-write. (2) THE CHANNEL: on the writer's answer to a message-write the api puts one notice {:agent k :t t} per recipient on its :messages out-port, the dedicated channel; the reconciler's :wake (today the same answer's :wake list) is one reader of it. (3) THE WATCH: a reader of that channel for one agent, held in the api's state as {k [watch ...]} (today's ::waits, keyed by agent, with the call's bound held there: await-message's :k :ms :due); a notice for k answers its watches {:t}; nothing is matched on commits. (4) THE READ, in the tools agents already use, no new op: seon.agent/messages (an agent's own REPL read, and a session's through seon_eval on its agent) and seon_inbox take since=<t>; the read is a query, the messages whose :seon.message/to link tx is past t, and :t, main's t it read; it writes nothing. With nothing past t it holds a watch, and on its notice queries since=t again. A model turn reads the same query from its last turn's basis. (5) A RESTART: the api's watches end typed at its stop (end-all, :seon.system/restarting with :next-port at a switch-over), and their callers ask again with their own since; a notice lost in flight costs nothing, since the query reads the facts; the new reconciler derives each parked agent's owed wake from facts (a message linked after its last turn's basis), so :late, settle-late and boot-wakes go. Open for the owner: removal (an agent retracting its ref) is a mark too. THE PUSH (the owner, 2026-11-16): seon_inbox, improved, takes stream=true and writes each new message as one line on its open connection, run by a Claude session under its Monitor tool; no new op or tool (the owner: use and improve the tools Seon agents use). It is the first consumer of seon.web/the-system-seen-from-flow's one stream, so it waits for it. A read-only look as a new op was built and reverted (jolt/flow ba6edc1bbc) for that reason.\nCOST: a read is one :avet seek of the agent's links and a tx filter, O(m) for m messages to it (~10.5k ticks a message [M]); no write. Lower bound: one seek.\nDELETES, with what does each job: seon.agent/deliver!'s message half and the branch copies (a turn reads main at its heard commit), seon.task/undelivered (the link-tx comparison), inbox-deliver and its live-process path (the read needs none), the reconciler's :late, settle-late and boot-wakes (the comparison derives owed), inbox-answer's volatile! and promise (the api's bound). No functionality removed but removal, which is the owner's call.\nSTEP 1 BUILT (flow, 2026-11-16): seon_inbox reads since=t and writes nothing, with no live process, its bound held by the api, and its wait answered by the commit's ops naming the agent (O(d) a commit); it completed an-inbox-answers-a-message-once, an-inbox-on-an-ended-agent-says-so and an-inbox-wait-holds-no-state-of-its-own. STEP 2 BUILT: stream=true on seon_inbox writes each message as a line through seon.server/*emit* as it comes, and bin/mcp call relays the lines, so a session's Monitor tool gets one notification a message. FRICTION SEEN (flow, 2026-11-16): a stream ends at each 10-minute wait with nothing (inbox-wait-ms), so every session re-arms its Monitor every 10 minutes; better: the stream waits on across bounds and writes one {:type :waiting :t} line at each, which ends it when its client has gone (the write fails), so only the Monitor's own 30-minute expiry re-arms. NEXT: a model turn reads the same query and deliver!'s message half, undelivered and the reconciler's :late go, completing a-message-is-delivered-only-by-its-delivery.\nPROOFS, red first: seon.server-test/an-inbox-answers-a-message-once-and-writes-nothing; seon.server-test/an-inbox-reads-an-ended-agents-messages; seon.reconcile-test/a-parked-agent-owed-a-message-is-woken-after-a-restart."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/an-inbox-answers-a-message-once-and-writes-nothing seon.server-test/an-inbox-reads-an-ended-agents-messages seon.reconcile-test/a-parked-agent-owed-a-message-is-woken-after-a-restart}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.web/the-system-seen-from-flow"}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.server{inbox-answer {:n 30, :order :linear, :ticks 400000, :bytes 3000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-refused-attach-names-its-branchs-unmerged-entries"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: web; data reviews. Found with data on root's seon_done pilot, 2026-11-16: attach of session/root was refused :seon.process/env-mismatch. The writer's :spawn merges main into a resumed branch before the fork (writer.clj:468-472), but its merge met conflicts (data: ~130 entries at derived keys, fixed on jolt/data 50e6939340; five real ones remain), so the branch kept its pins and the agent refused to start. The refusal names the mismatch, not its cause.\nPRIOR WORK, searched (seon.writer :spawn's :merged, seon.system's spawn reply, which keeps only an error at system.clj:879; seon.merge/unmerged, my.branch/unmerged): the unmerged entries are facts on the branch, read by seon.merge/unmerged.\nTHE CHANGE: seon.server/attach!'s answer, refused or not, carries the branch's unmerged entries (:unmerged, their keys, from seon.merge/unmerged on the branch's db) whenever there are any, with the one repair when its agent cannot start: root resolves each entry from its REPL (docs/merging.md#root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl: seon.merge/side-tx and resolve-tx through the writer, then :merge-main), and the session attaches again. Nothing new is stored and nothing is checked at a caller: the answer reads what the branch holds.\nCOST: one seon.merge/unmerged read of the branch a refused or resumed attach, O(entries); lower bound the entries' seek.\nPROOF, red first: seon.server-test/a-refused-attach-names-its-branchs-unmerged-entries: a branch holding one unmerged entry, its attach refused; the answer names the entry's key. Red (2 fail: no :unmerged, no repair) then green on jolt/web, 607k ticks against 3M; refusals only (a started agent reads (my.branch/unmerged) itself)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.server-test/a-refused-attach-names-its-branchs-unmerged-entries}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.server{attach! {:n 10, :order :linear, :ticks 200000, :bytes 1000000}}
